t least um this is  probably an 80  solution maybe more and there's  because we're not transforming the data  at any point  during that stream a whole lot of the  volume of code in like a rails app or a  node.js  app or something is about you know  hiding a field as it comes out of the  database before it goes to the api or  adding a new field when it comes in  via the api on the way to the database  and you don't do that and so  there's a lot less code so we'll drill  into  a couple of the big sections of this api  um  oh i'm really going to have to to go  fast after this  so here if a document is a task  we're going to say it has to have a list  id and if it doesn't we're going to  reject that update and it won't sync up  from the cloud  from the client so yeah you can hack  your client to do whatever crazy stuff  but it's not going to  go through our cloud to other clients  and then the last thing we'll do is  we'll take that lit  that that task item and we'll stick it  on the appropriate list for that list id  so that's pretty simple this is a little  bit more interesting  so not only does the sync function is it  able to route data to the appropriate  channels but it can  modify the access grants for a channel  so in this case we have a document that  is  of type list and so it has to have an  owner  and that require user line kind of  halfway down  says that if somebody's modifying this  list document it better be the owner of  the previous version of that list  document  and then the last bit of work it does  is make sure that anyone who's listed as  a member  can synchronize all the tasks that are  associated with that list  as well as putting this meta document on  that channel  as well so that you know the users can  see who the other members are  etc so with those  moving parts and you know there's a  little little bit more api surface area  that's available to you if you need it  the hope is that you should be able to  build something you know  almost as complex as a facebook or an  instagram or something  uh with you know maybe if you built all  of facebook it would be 500 lines of  code  instead of 20 but the idea is that you  know this should give you the moving  parts that you need in order to  build your back end and you're not  fussing around with you know crud and  request response cycles in your  application server  so yeah i'll save questions for that to  the end because i want to go off the  deep end here  and talk about the data structures that  you can use for conflict detection and  management  so vector clocks are well known and  popular we don't use them  we don't use them because they scale  with the number of processes that are  updating a given data item  so you know i also find them a little  bit hard to reason about  because you know whether or not  something is a conflict is  always relative to something else so in  this case  the updates those boxes that are in the  the non-shaded area  are conflicts relative to the update  that's right in the middle  of the of the graph there  and you know there are ways to prune  vector clocks so you can make up for  some of the scalability problems but  they're really  they're designed for systems where you  have a bounded number of clients  and you know kind of like how big your  system is in general  which you just don't know i mean you  hope you don't know how many users of  your mobile app you're going to have  when you launch it  so the other big key thing that we want  to do  is never throw data away i mentioned  that at the beginning so  uh that means that you know some of the  sync apis i've looked at  uh your client you know launches and  gets an input stream of some stuff from  the cloud  and you know some of that conflicts with  local updates that have been made in the  meantime  and you have to decide right then like  merge it or drop one  and that's that's your option it's  much i think more developer friendly to  give you also the option to do nothing  just defer the decision wait until you  know there's not just two conflicting  branches of a document but three or four  or five you know lots of opinions  and maybe merge on the client for  certain fields that need  human eyes to merge and have a script in  the cloud that merges  you know for things that can be  automated and so  uh by you know tracking um  instead of vector clocks which are kind  of based on who did it  we're more interested in what did they  do sort of content based  and so by tracking it in that way it  becomes cheaper to  never throw data away and allow you to  defer those decisions until you're  in the right position to do it so here  is i guess the glossary for this data  structure section  um logically the way we track each  revision of a document  you know each revision has a parent uh  revision  is kind of like git and so you've got a  generation counter  and then you've got a content hash and  the content hash is only a content hash  to allow us to do some certain  optimizations it could be randomly  generated but a content hash is better  and so with uh the rest of the glossary  is that  well on the wire in real life these look  like three dash  you know some content hash stuff  but for purposes of displaying it on the  screen and apologies in advance to  colorblind people but  i've mapped the hash numbers to  colors on the color wheel and so you  know  higher numbers are lighter let's say and  and lower numbers are darker  and the two other things you need to  know is that  anything in a yellow circle is the de  facto winner  and anything in a gray circle is a  conflicting version that we're not going  to throw away  until the user tells us to so when you  start out  it's as simple as i've got one client  who has the first generation of the  document  and there's lots of things that could  play out here but i played out a  scenario that illustrates the scaling  differences between this  and a vector clock so  we're going to synchronize that over to  the  to the other client and so now they both  agree about the state of the universe  and the second client is going to make  an update  so now the second client can garbage  collect  you know the first generation's body and  all it keeps is the metadata about that  so  so the only document bodies that are  still  existing are the first generation on one  client and the second generation another  client  will push back uh replication the other  way  and now the uh now they both agree right  it's pretty trivial there's no conflict  there because only one of them was  editing at any given time  and we'll just do that again so we'll  have this kind of ping pong scenario  and you get a document that is  three generations old and only has  uh you know one live copy source of  truth right because there's just no  conflicts to worry about  so the third generation is is the real  deal but um  your uh you know both your phones went  on different airplane rides or hey the  same airplane ride i don't know  they're disconnected for long enough for  both users to do some work and so one of  them does some purple work another one  does some green work  and now we have a conflict to deal with  what are we going to do when they learn  about  each other's stuff  well we'll synchronize that over there  and  in this case we've decided that green is  lighter colored so it's going to win  so the lighter colored one is the  provisional winner but we don't throw  away  the gray one we keep it around so you  can merge it later if you want to  we also if we were to say like hey keep  them both around and assign them equal  precedence  that's the same thing as forcing you to  deal with the conflict right away and  not allowing you to defer the decision  because  any get from your application is going  to get two versions and now all your  application code  is always having this error handling you  know threaded throughout it what happens  if i get two versions so we don't do  that  we if if you don't ask for conflicts we  just give you the yellow one  but we give you the same yellow one on  every device  so now the devices agree and they didn't  have to talk to agree because they just  picked the one that had the higher hash  value  for its content so  the this gives you this gives you this  distributed agreement but  you know now we have two things to keep  track of because we have two different  edits and we don't want to throw one  away  so let's bring in uh another client  that looks like it diverged back in the  third generation  of updates and what's going to happen  when that propagates back through  the the mesh of piers  well it turns out that that blue 4  that the client that was kind of long  lost and came back had created  is an even higher hash value than the  green 4  and so it becomes the winner and this  phone in the middle now knows  about you know three alternate realities  for the fourth generation of this  document and it's going to hang on to  the gray ones and present you  the yellow one if you you know don't  tell it that you  you know that you're aware of conflicts  and just to play this out a little bit  farther  let's say the user on this first phone  actually resolved a conflict they said  you know what that purple 4  that i don't need that anymore let's  throw that out i'll make a new  generation  based on you know the green four and so  now i have you know a five  um and i have a tombstone on four  so when that synchronizes back uh to  the client that you know has has learned  more than anybody else  at this point that tombstone  synchronizes  and five becomes the provisional winner  even though it's a darker color  than uh than the blue four because it's  a later generation  and so because we always give precedence  to  the latest generation you get uh  more or less read your own rights  because if you do a right it's going to  be  the child of whatever the previous  conflict winner was and so unless  someone else is doing rights  aggressively and synchronizing them in  like you know while you're in your ui  flow or something  you're going to read your own rights  because you always wrote the thing with  the highest generation number  so to play this out a little bit more  let's say now the um that long lost  client  gets a replication from the uh you know  the one that knows everything  and now they also agree and not only  they agree but the long-lost client  never  even had to learn about the four that  has the tombstone on it it's just  you know it's irrelevant so it didn't  get synced over  and uh you know the last thing that  we're going to want to do  eventually this chain of metadata you  know you want to prune it  just like you know any of these data  structures that  any kind of log that can grow without  bound but as long as we're kind of  you know pruning stuff that's old enough  not to matter for  your merge strategy then  pruning is relatively harmless so we're  able to  you know drop those old generations off  and and still give you you know more or  less what you would need in order to  correctly detect conflicts and merge  them  so for our use case we chose these  revision trees over vector clocks  vector clocks may be better in a  constrained environment like a data  center but  when you're fully disconnected you know  i think  revision trees are a better fit so  if you want to try this out  mobile.couchbase.com has all the  downloads  and you know find me afterward or  jayce on twitter or just start shouting  couchbase on twitter and i'll hear it  and  come help you or take feedback or  whatever so thank you
 

